Making the x86-64 guest actually cooperative
=============================================

This is the plan for turning the current single-stepped bring-up into a real
cooperative guest, modelled on what coLinux i386 does. It is written after
reading the upstream source (colinux/patch/base-2.6.33.diff and
colinux/src/colinux/kernel/monitor.c), not from guessing, and every mechanism
below has a named counterpart there.


0. Where we are, and why it is not cooperative
----------------------------------------------

The guest boots. It reaches do_idle at ~170M instructions, halts, and the host
stops. Getting there costs ~90 seconds of one core pinned at 100% inside a
driver ioctl, because every guest instruction is single-stepped: TF is set, the
CPU traps after each instruction, and the host re-arms it. 170M traps is 170M
world-switch-sized events.

That is the opposite of cooperative in two ways:

  * it takes total control of a core for the whole run, and
  * at idle it terminates the guest rather than handing the CPU back and
    resuming later when there is work.

Single-stepping was the right scaffold for an unbootable guest -- it bounds a
guest that would otherwise run away with interrupts off, and it turns every
fault into a reportable stop. It has done its job. A booting guest does not
need it, and cannot afford it.


1. The coLinux cooperative model, from the source
-------------------------------------------------

The guest runs FREE, at native speed, with real interrupts enabled. It does not
get stepped. It hands control back to the host by *calling the world switch
itself* at a small number of cooperative points. Between those points it runs
like any kernel.

Three yield points, all of which call co_switch_wrapper() -- the guest half of
the passage-page world switch:

  a. Idle. arch/x86/kernel/process.c points pm_idle at co_idle_processor:

         local_irq_disable();
         co_passage_page->operation = CO_OPERATION_IDLE;
         co_switch_wrapper();          /* hand the CPU to the host */
         co_callback(NULL);            /* on re-entry, absorb what host sent */
         local_irq_enable();

     No hlt. The guest yields; the host runs its own world (Windows, other
     work); it re-enters the guest when there is something to do, and the guest
     drains it in co_callback.

  b. A real host hardware interrupt arriving while the guest is current. Every
     IDT vector is pointed at proxy_interrupt_handler (i8259_cooperative.c):

         co_passage_page->operation = CO_OPERATION_FORWARD_INTERRUPT;
         co_passage_page->params[0] = vector;
         co_passage_page->host_state.flags &= ~(1 << 9);   /* IF off in host flags */
         co_switch_wrapper();
         co_callback(regs);

     The interrupt is not handled in the guest. It is handed to the host, which
     delivers it to Windows' own ISR -- see section 3 for the crucial detail of
     *where* that happens.

  c. Sending a message to the host: CO_OPERATION_MESSAGE_TO_MONITOR, for device
     I/O and console output.

Time is virtual. There is no guest timer hardware. The host tells the guest how
many ticks have passed by putting a count in the passage page, and
co_handle_jiffies delivers them by calling the guest's own timer IRQ that many
times:

         while (count-- > 0) { irq_enter(); __do_IRQ(TIMER_IRQ); irq_exit(); }

Devices are virtual too. A virtual interrupt controller, COPIC
(i8259_cooperative.c), replaces the 8259/APIC. Its "interrupts" are messages
the host queues; the guest delivers each with __do_IRQ(device_irq). The
per-device IRQ numbers (KEYBOARD_IRQ, NETWORK_IRQ, BLOCKDEV_IRQ, ...) are fixed.

The whole conversation runs over the passage page plus an I/O area, with a
small set of operations (from base-2.6.33.diff):

    START IDLE TERMINATE MESSAGE_TO_MONITOR MESSAGE_FROM_MONITOR
    FORWARD_INTERRUPT DEVICE GET_TIME GET_HIGH_PREC_TIME
    DEBUG_LINE TRACE_POINT FREE_PAGES ALLOC_PAGES GETPP

The host monitor loop (monitor.c: iteration()) is the mirror image: switch to
the guest, and on the switch back dispatch on co_passage_page->operation --
IDLE sleeps until a message arrives, FORWARD_INTERRUPT calls the host ISR,
MESSAGE_TO_MONITOR routes a device message, and so on, then switch back in.


2. What we have and what is missing
-----------------------------------

Have:
  * The world switch itself (co_switch_full), bidirectional, proven.
  * The passage page and guest address space.
  * The guest kernel patched enough to boot to idle.
  * Fault, warning, halt and exception-table handling for bring-up.

Missing -- this is the cooperative core, and it is most of M4/M5:
  * The passage-page message protocol: the operation dispatch, the I/O area,
    the message queues. We have `params[]` used ad hoc for the boot test; there
    is no operation loop.
  * Cooperative idle: co_idle_processor and the host co_idle that sleeps and
    wakes on a message. We have a double-ud2 halt that stops the run.
  * Virtual time: co_handle_jiffies driven by a host tick count. We have
    nothing; jiffies never advance, which is why the guest cannot leave idle.
  * Proxy interrupt forwarding done coLinux's way, plus the x64 CR8 fix
    (section 3).
  * COPIC and the device IRQ mapping.
  * The guest-side glue (co_switch_wrapper, co_callback, co_send_message) as
    x86-64 rather than the i386 in base-2.6.33.diff.

And the thing that goes away: single-stepping, TF re-arming, the batch, and the
per-instruction IF clearing. The guest runs free.


3. The x86-64 problem coLinux never had: CR8 / IRQL
---------------------------------------------------

Our earlier attempt to run with IF=1 and forward interrupts killed the host
every time. The switch's own comment records it: "every run that forwarded even
one interrupt died." That was read as "forwarding is fatal, keep IF=0." Reading
the coLinux source shows the opposite -- coLinux forwards constantly and
survives -- so the fatal part is specific to us, and there are two causes.

First, *where* the host ISR is called. coLinux calls Windows' ISR only after a
full cooperative world switch back to the host, in host context, on the host
stack, under the host CR3/GDT/IDT, via call_intr() building a synthetic
interrupt frame (interrupt.c). Our dormant path tried to call it from inside
the monitor ioctl on a hand-built frame without having cleanly returned to host
context. Same idea, wrong place.

Second, and specific to 64-bit: CR8 is the task priority register, and on x64
Windows CR8 *is* IRQL. A hardware interrupt raises it; the ISR lowers it on the
way out. Our world switch carries CR0, CR3, CR4, the MSRs, the segment
selectors, GDT, IDT and TR -- but not CR8. So an interrupt taken on the guest
side, forwarded, and completed on the host side leaves the CPU's task priority
inconsistent with what Windows believes IRQL to be, and the machine dies later,
unrelated -- the exact signature we kept seeing. i386 has no CR8; IRQL there is
the PIC mask and the HAL's software level, so coLinux never had to carry it.
CR8 must travel across the switch, both directions, like CR3 does.

The host self-check must grow a CR8 field too, so a future omission is caught at
the crossing rather than as a delayed freeze.


4. The build, in order, each piece testable on its own
------------------------------------------------------

Stage A -- the operation loop and cooperative idle (no interrupts yet)
    Give the passage page a real operation field and an I/O area. Replace the
    monitor's step loop with an operation loop: enter the guest, and on the
    switch back dispatch on operation. Implement START, IDLE, TERMINATE,
    MESSAGE_TO_MONITOR (printk only, to keep the console), and GETPP.

    Patch the guest: co_switch_wrapper / co_callback / co_send_message as
    x86-64; point pm_idle (select_idle_routine) at co_idle_processor. Drop the
    double-ud2 halt.

    The guest still runs free but with interrupts *masked at the source* -- keep
    IF clear for this stage by not yet enabling the proxy path, so nothing
    depends on CR8/forwarding. The bound is now the guest's own cooperative
    yields (idle) plus faults, not stepping. This is where we find out whether
    the boot has any genuinely unbounded non-yielding loop; the trace-eval one
    we "fixed" was a stepping artifact and should be reverted and retested here,
    because at native speed it is nothing.

    Done when: the guest boots to idle in well under a second, yields
    CO_OPERATION_IDLE, the host re-enters, and printk still reaches the console
    over MESSAGE_TO_MONITOR.

Stage B -- virtual time
    Host keeps a tick count (from its own timer, or a coarse wall-clock read per
    idle) and hands it to the guest on each re-entry. Implement co_handle_jiffies
    and wire TIMER_IRQ. Now jiffies advance, the scheduler runs, and the guest
    leaves idle on its own -- init runs.

    Done when: the guest's uptime advances, and it proceeds past "reached idle"
    into running the init process.

Stage C -- CR8 across the switch, then proxy interrupt forwarding
    Add CR8 to the state block, the switch save/restore (both directions), and
    the host self-check. Only then bring up proxy_interrupt_handler: point the
    guest IDT vectors at it, run the guest with real IF=1, and on
    CO_OPERATION_FORWARD_INTERRUPT deliver the host ISR the coLinux way
    (call_intr equivalent, after the switch back). Test with one forwarded
    interrupt first, with the self-check watching CR8 and the segments, before
    trusting it under load.

    Done when: a run survives thousands of forwarded host interrupts with the
    self-check clean every crossing.

Stage D -- the COPIC and device messages
    The virtual controller, the device IRQ map, MESSAGE_FROM_MONITOR delivering
    queued device messages via __do_IRQ. This is what makes cobd (block) and
    conet (net) and the cooperative console work -- i.e. a usable machine.


5. What stays true throughout
-----------------------------

  * The host must come back from every crossing exactly as it left. The
    self-check is the guarantor; it grows CR8 and the segments in this work, and
    a run stops at the first divergence rather than freezing later.
  * The guest is trusted. Cooperative Linux is not a hypervisor: a guest that
    runs away without yielding can take the host, and that is accepted, the same
    way it is accepted upstream. The bound is the guest's own cooperative
    yields, not a hardware watchdog.
  * Every stage is verified against the coLinux reference, which is a working
    implementation of exactly this, one architecture over.
